(>> Solution to Question 1:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the fundamental principles of orthographic projection, detailing how a three-dimensional object is mapped onto two-dimensional planes using parallel normal projecting rays.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Theoretical Foundation of Orthographic Projection:) Tj
T*
(   - Orthographic projection is a parallel projection technique in which the lines of sight) Tj
T*
(   \(projecting rays\) are mutually parallel to each other and intersect the projection plane at) Tj
T*
(   strictly right angles \(90 degrees / normal incidence\).) Tj
T*
(   - Because the projecting rays are parallel, the distance between the observer and the object is) Tj
T*
(   theoretically infinite. Consequently, there is no perspective foreshortening or vanishing point) Tj
T*
(   distortion; features parallel to the projection plane are depicted in their TRUE geometric size) Tj
T*
(   and shape.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. The Orthogonal Projection Box \(Glass Box Concept\):) Tj
T*
(   - Imagine an object suspended inside a transparent rectangular glass box comprising six) Tj
T*
(   mutually perpendicular faces.) Tj
T*
(   - Each face of the glass box serves as an orthogonal projection plane:) Tj
T*
(   * Frontal Plane \(Vertical Plane - VP\) -> Captures Front Elevation.) Tj
T*
(   * Horizontal Plane \(HP\) -> Captures Plan / Top View.) Tj
T*
(   * Profile Planes \(Left and Right - PP\) -> Capture Side Views.) Tj
T*
(   * Bottom Plane -> Captures Bottom View.) Tj
T*
(   * Rear Plane -> Captures Rear Elevation.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Unfolding the Glass Box into a 2D Drawing Sheet:) Tj
T*
(   - To convert these six spatial views into a single flat drawing sheet:) Tj
T*
(   * The Frontal Plane is held stationary as the primary anchor.) Tj
T*
(   * The Horizontal Plane is hinged and rotated 90 degrees to become coplanar with the Frontal) Tj
T*
(   Plane.) Tj
T*
(   * The Side Profile Planes are hinged along the vertical edges of the Frontal Plane and swung) Tj
T*
(   open 90 degrees like doors until flat.) Tj
T*
(   - This geometric flattening preserves exact coordinate alignments between views \(e.g. width) Tj
T*
(   aligns vertically between front and top views; height aligns horizontally between front and) Tj
T*
(   side views\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 2:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Compare and contrast First Angle Projection and Third Angle Projection systems in exhaustive detail, including relative positions of views, international symbols, and regional adoption.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. First Angle Projection System:) Tj
T*
(   - Principle: The object is placed in the First Quadrant, situated directly BETWEEN the observer) Tj
T*
(   and the projection plane.) Tj
T*
(   - Projection Mechanics: The observer looks through the object; the rays hit the object first,) Tj
T*
(   projecting its shadow/silhouette onto the opaque plane behind it.) Tj
T*
(   - Arrangement of Views on Sheet:) Tj
T*
(   * Front View \(Elevation\) is the central reference.) Tj
T*
(   * Top View \(Plan\) is drawn BELOW the front view.) Tj
T*
(   * Left Hand Side View \(LHSV\) is drawn on the RIGHT of the front view.) Tj
T*
(   * Right Hand Side View \(RHSV\) is drawn on the LEFT of the front view.) Tj
T*
(   * Bottom View is drawn ABOVE the front view.) Tj
T*
(   - Graphical Symbol: Frustum of a cone positioned with its tapered trapezoidal profile on the) Tj
T*
(   left, and two concentric circles \(representing the small and large ends\) on the right.) Tj
T*
ET

