(>> Solution to Question 3:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Detail the six standard principal views of an object in orthographic projection, explaining their geometric relationships and the dimensional alignment between them.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. The Six Principal Orthographic Views:) Tj
T*
(   - 1. Front View \(Front Elevation\): View obtained by looking at the object from the front.) Tj
T*
(   Displays WIDTH \(X\) and HEIGHT \(Z\).) Tj
T*
(   - 2. Top View \(Plan\): View obtained by looking vertically downward from above. Displays WIDTH) Tj
T*
(   \(X\) and DEPTH \(Y\).) Tj
T*
(   - 3. Left Hand Side View \(End View\): View obtained by looking from the left side. Displays) Tj
T*
(   DEPTH \(Y\) and HEIGHT \(Z\).) Tj
T*
(   - 4. Right Hand Side View \(End View\): View obtained by looking from the right side. Displays) Tj
T*
(   DEPTH \(Y\) and HEIGHT \(Z\).) Tj
T*
(   - 5. Bottom View: View obtained by looking vertically upward from underneath. Displays WIDTH) Tj
T*
(   \(X\) and DEPTH \(Y\).) Tj
T*
(   - 6. Rear View \(Back Elevation\): View obtained by looking from behind the object. Displays) Tj
T*
(   WIDTH \(X\) and HEIGHT \(Z\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Dimensional Alignment and Correspondence:) Tj
T*
(   - Width Correspondence \(X-axis\):) Tj
T*
(   The Front View, Top View, and Bottom View share the exact same horizontal width. Projector) Tj
T*
(   lines connecting these views must be strictly vertical and collinear.) Tj
T*
(   - Height Correspondence \(Z-axis\):) Tj
T*
(   The Front View, Left Side View, Right Side View, and Rear View share the exact same vertical) Tj
T*
(   height. Projector lines connecting these views are strictly horizontal.) Tj
T*
(   - Depth Correspondence \(Y-axis\):) Tj
T*
(   The Top View, Bottom View, Left Side View, and Right Side View share the exact same depth) Tj
T*
(   dimension. Depth is transferred between top and side views using a 45-degree miter line or) Tj
T*
(   compass swing arcs.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Rule of Selection:) Tj
T*
(   In engineering practice, an object is rarely drawn with all six views. The three standard views) Tj
T*
(   \(Front View, Top View, and one Side View\) are usually sufficient to define its complete) Tj
T*
(   geometry.) Tj
T*
ET

endstream
