(>> Solution to Question 4:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the methods of transferring depth dimensions between the Top View and Side Views in manual and CAD drafting: the 45-degree Miter Line Method and the Compass Arc Method.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Depth Transfer Principle:) Tj
T*
(   In orthographic layout, the Top View displays width horizontally and depth vertically. The Side) Tj
T*
(   View displays depth horizontally and height vertically.) Tj
T*
(   Therefore, the vertical depth dimension from the Top View must be converted into a horizontal) Tj
T*
(   depth dimension for the Side View without introducing measurement errors.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. The 45-Degree Miter Line Method:) Tj
T*
(   - Construction Steps:) Tj
T*
(   * Step 1: Draw the Front View and Top View in vertical alignment separated by a spacing) Tj
T*
(   distance 'S'.) Tj
T*
(   * Step 2: Establish the intersection origin O between the horizontal extension of the bottom of) Tj
T*
(   the Front View \(or reference line XY\) and the vertical projector of the left/right edge of the) Tj
T*
(   Side View.) Tj
T*
(   * Step 3: From origin O, draw a straight miter line inclined at exactly 45 degrees into the) Tj
T*
(   quadrant separating the views.) Tj
T*
(   * Step 4: From every feature vertex in the Top View, project horizontal lines to the right) Tj
T*
(   until they intersect the 45-degree miter line.) Tj
T*
(   * Step 5: At each intersection point on the miter line, project lines vertically upward into) Tj
T*
(   the Side View region.) Tj
T*
(   * Step 6: Project horizontal lines from the Front View across to intersect these vertical) Tj
T*
(   projectors. The intersection grid defines the exact vertices of the Side View!) Tj
T*
(   - Mathematical Validity:) Tj
T*
(   In an isosceles right triangle with 45? base angles, horizontal leg equals vertical leg \(dx =) Tj
T*
(   dy\). Thus, depth is transferred with 100% geometric fidelity.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. The Compass Arc Method:) Tj
T*
(   - Rather than a 45? line, a compass with center at origin O is used to swing 90-degree circular) Tj
T*
(   arcs transferring depth coordinates from the horizontal axis to the vertical axis.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 5:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Discuss the rules and conventions for representing hidden features \(dashed lines\) in orthographic drawings according to BIS SP 46 and ISO 128, including intersection etiquette.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Purpose and Standard Definition:) Tj
T*
(   - Hidden lines \(Type E or F, ~0.25 mm thin\) are used to represent edges, intersections,) Tj
T*
(   cavities, or surfaces that are obscured from the observer's line of sight by intervening solid) Tj
T*
(   material.) Tj
T*
(   - Dash Geometry: Nominal dash length is 3 mm to 4 mm, with uniform inter-dash spacing of 1 mm.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Intersection and Termination Rules \(Drafting Etiquette\):) Tj
T*
(   - Rule 1 \(Intersection with Visible Lines\):) Tj
T*
(   When a hidden line terminates at or originates from a visible outline, the first dash MUST) Tj
T*
(   physically touch the visible line without any gap. \(Exception: If the hidden line is a) Tj
T*
(   continuation of a visible line, leave a 1 mm gap to indicate a change of edge state\).) Tj
T*
(   - Rule 2 \(Hidden Corners and Vertices\):) Tj
T*
(   When two hidden lines meet to form an angle or corner, the dashes must join solidly at the) Tj
T*
(   vertex without an empty space.) Tj
T*
ET

