12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the difference between First Angle and Third Angle projection symbols, providing exact geometric proportions for drawing them according to BIS SP 46.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Geometry of the Projection Symbol:) Tj
T*
(   - Both First and Third Angle projection symbols are constructed from a standardized frustum of) Tj
T*
(   a right circular cone accompanied by two concentric circles representing its end views.) Tj
T*
(   - Standard Dimension Proportions \(based on large diameter D\):) Tj
T*
(   * Large Diameter: D \(e.g. 10 mm\).) Tj
T*
(   * Small Diameter: d = 0.5 * D \(e.g. 5 mm\).) Tj
T*
(   * Axial Length of Frustum: L = D \(e.g. 10 mm\).) Tj
T*
(   * Spacing between frustum and concentric circles: S = D \(e.g. 10 mm\).) Tj
T*
(   * Concentric circles diameters: d \(5 mm\) and D \(10 mm\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. First Angle Projection Symbol Layout:) Tj
T*
(   - The tapered trapezoid \(frustum profile\) is placed on the LEFT.) Tj
T*
(   * Its large vertical face \(D\) is on the left, small face \(d\) is on the right.) Tj
T*
(   - The two concentric circles are placed on the RIGHT.) Tj
T*
(   - Explanation: An observer looking from the left sees the large base first; the projection of) Tj
T*
(   the small base onto the right plane represents First Angle viewing.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Third Angle Projection Symbol Layout:) Tj
T*
(   - The two concentric circles are placed on the LEFT.) Tj
T*
(   - The tapered trapezoid \(frustum profile\) is placed on the RIGHT, with its small face \(d\)) Tj
T*
(   facing towards the circles.) Tj
T*
(   - Explanation: In Third Angle projection, looking from the left projects the view onto the left) Tj
T*
(   plane facing the observer.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   4. Title Block Placement:) Tj
T*
(   - The symbol is drafted at the designated cell inside the Title Block \(typically within a 45 mm) Tj
T*
(   x 20 mm area\) on layer '0_Border' or 'Annotations' with 0.25 mm lineweight.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 23:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the methods of drawing runouts and fillet blend transitions in orthographic views, detailing when lines are drawn and when they are omitted.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Engineering Nature of Fillet Blends:) Tj
T*
(   - In cast, forged, and molded engineering parts, sharp interior and exterior corners are) Tj
T*
(   avoided to eliminate stress concentrations and facilitate molten metal flow. Instead, edges are) Tj
T*
(   rounded with fillets and rounds.) Tj
T*
(   - Because curved surfaces blend tangentially into adjacent flat planar faces without a sharp) Tj
T*
(   angular corner, no true mathematical line of intersection exists.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET

