T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the procedure to create an aligned Auxiliary View for an object having an inclined face, detailing why principal views fail to show the true shape of inclined surfaces.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Limitation of Principal Orthographic Views:) Tj
T*
(   - When an object contains a planar surface inclined to both the horizontal and profile planes,) Tj
T*
(   its projections on the standard Front, Top, and Side views are FORESHORTENED.) Tj
T*
(   - The interior angles and lengths do not display true physical values; circular holes on the) Tj
T*
(   face project as distorted ellipses.) Tj
T*
(   - To show the TRUE SHAPE of such a surface for dimensional callouts and tooling, an AUXILIARY) Tj
T*
(   VIEW must be projected onto an Auxiliary Plane parallel to the inclined face!) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Step-by-Step Auxiliary View Construction in AutoCAD:) Tj
T*
(   - Step 1: In the Front View, identify the edge view of the inclined surface \(where the surface) Tj
T*
(   appears as a single straight sloping line segment AB\).) Tj
T*
(   - Step 2: Establish User Coordinate System \(UCS\) aligned with the inclined surface:) Tj
T*
(   * Command: UCS -> 3P \(Origin at A, positive X-axis along edge AB\).) Tj
T*
(   - Step 3: Draw construction rays \(XLINE -> Ver\) perpendicular to edge AB projecting outward) Tj
T*
(   into empty drawing space.) Tj
T*
(   - Step 4: Establish a reference datum line parallel to edge AB.) Tj
T*
(   - Step 5: Transfer depth dimensions of all feature vertices from the Top View along the) Tj
T*
(   corresponding projection rays.) Tj
T*
(   - Step 6: Connect the plotted points. Circular holes on the inclined face are drawn as TRUE) Tj
T*
(   CIRCLES in this auxiliary view!) Tj
T*
(   - Step 7: Reset coordinate system via UCS -> World \(W\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
